Modifications by the streetCryptid project
------------------------------------------

In accordance with section 5 of the GNU AGPL, the changes made to the upstream
source after it was vendored into this repository are dated and described
below. Entries before 2026-11-02 describe changes to v0.3.1; the 2026-11-02
entry says which of them survive on top of v0.5.1-beta.7.

2026-07 — Ported from iroh 0.98.2 to iroh 1.0.2, and made buildable as a
standalone / path dependency:

  * Cargo.toml
      - Bumped dependencies to the versions used by this app's iroh 1.0 stack:
        `iroh` 0.98.2 -> 1.0.2 (feature `unstable-custom-transports`),
        `iroh-base` 0.98 -> 1.0.2, `n0-watcher` 0.6 -> 1, `noq-udp` 0.10 -> 1,
        `noq-proto` 0.17 -> 1.
      - Removed workspace inheritance (`rust-version.workspace = true` ->
        `rust-version = "1.95"`) so the crate builds outside the upstream
        workspace; added `publish = false`.
      - Added this vendoring/modification header and updated the description.

  * src/transport/hook.rs — updated to the iroh 1.0 `EndpointHooks` API:
      - `after_handshake` now receives `&iroh::endpoint::Connection` instead of
        the removed `&ConnectionInfo`.
      - Dropped the `!path.is_closed()` filter: `Connection::paths()` in 1.0
        only returns currently-open paths and `Path::is_closed` no longer
        exists.
      - Switched the connection-close watcher from a cloned `Connection` to a
        `WeakConnectionHandle` (`conn.weak_handle().closed().await`), which the
        1.0 hook documentation requires so the hook does not keep the
        connection alive / disable close-on-drop.

  * src/transport/transport.rs — updated to the iroh 1.0
    `CustomEndpoint` / `CustomSender` traits:
      - `poll_recv` now fills a `&mut [RecvInfo]` slice, constructing entries
        with `RecvInfo::new(custom_addr, None)`, instead of writing
        `Addr::Custom(..)` into a `&mut [Addr]` slice.
      - `poll_send` gained the new `src: Option<&CustomAddr>` argument (unused
        by this single-homed transport).
      - Updated the corresponding imports and a unit test call site, plus a
        stale doc comment referencing the iroh version.

2026-07 — Hardened streetCryptid's foreground nearby-pairing bootstrap:

  * src/transport/transport.rs
      - Added a static, read-only full EndpointId characteristic to the transport's own primary
         GATT service so identity resolution survives adapter/GATT-server rebuilds.
      - Exported helpers that validate a resolved full EndpointId against the 12-byte prefix in its
         advertised key UUID.

  * src/transport/mod.rs and src/lib.rs
      - Re-exported the identity characteristic UUID and prefix-validation helpers for the app's
         explicit RSSI-ranked Bump resolver.

  * src/transport/registry.rs
      - Treats the local close of an identity-probe connection while a peer is still `Discovered`
         as informational, preserving the immediately subsequent iroh dial instead of poisoning the
         route through `Draining` / `Dead`.

  * src/transport/events.rs and src/transport/transport.rs
      - Added a short-lived, per-device marker for app-owned identity-probe disconnects. This
         suppresses CoreBluetooth callbacks that arrive after the real pairing dial has already
         advanced the peer beyond `Discovered`.

2026-10 — A BLE pipe outlives its last iroh connection, and a simulated radio to test that on:

  * src/transport/hook.rs
      - A pipe whose last watched connection closed is released only after `PIPE_IDLE_LINGER`
         (30 s) with no new connection on it, instead of at once. Pairing sends each message on
         its own short connection, and the next message's dial raced the teardown of the pipe it
         was about to use.
      - The connection tracking moved to the transport, so every hook it hands out shares it.

  * src/transport/routing.rs and src/transport/transport.rs (`poll_send`)
      - A peer keeps its `StableConnId` across pipes: once a pipe to it is gone, the next pipe to
         the same peer opens under the same id (from the resolver, or from a send iroh makes on
         the old id), because iroh keeps dialling a peer on the id it was first given. An id stays
         dead while its peer has a live route under another id, or is out of scan range.

  * src/transport/registry.rs
      - A send that asks for a new pipe redials a `Dead { Forgotten }` peer (its pipe idled out or
         drained) instead of being refused until garbage collection, and one that arrives while
         the old pipe drains is held and dialled when the drain ends. `MaxRetries` and
         `ProtocolMismatch` stay dead.

  * src/transport/transport.rs
      - Split the registry/driver/hook/watchdog wiring out of `construct` into `assemble`, which
         takes any `BleInterface`, so the simulator runs the same code as a phone.

  * src/transport/sim.rs (new, `testing` feature) and tests/sim_pairing.rs (new)
      - `SimRadio`: an in-process radio that delivers GATT writes and notifications between
         nodes' registries with ordered latency, link loss, and a log of every link opened, so
         real iroh endpoints can run over the BLE transport alone.

  * Cargo.toml
      - Enabled tokio's `test-util` for the paused-clock hook tests.

2026-10 — A link can be kept for a few protocols:

  * src/transport/priority.rs (new), src/transport/hook.rs and src/transport/transport.rs
      - `BleTransport::reserve_links_for(alpns, held)`: while `held()` is true, the dedup hook
         refuses any other protocol on BLE — `before_connect` rejects a dial to a peer in BLE
         range, and `after_handshake` closes a connection that arrived over BLE with
         `BLE_HELD_CLOSE_CODE`. IP and relay connections are untouched. streetCryptid holds the
         link for its pairing protocol during a Bump window, after friend sync crowded a pairing
         message off a pipe.

  * tests/sim_pairing.rs
      - Tests that held links refuse other protocols in both directions and release afterwards.

2026-10-31 — A hold also closes connections already on the link:

  * src/transport/priority.rs, src/transport/hook.rs and src/transport/transport.rs
      - The hook tracks every connection it admits over BLE (weakly), and
        `reserve_links_for` starts a 500 ms sweep that, while the hold is on, closes
        tracked connections of other protocols that still ride BLE (`BLE_HELD_CLOSE_CODE`).
        Friend sync established before a Bump window opened kept streaming through it and
        delayed every pairing message behind ~700 KB of traffic.

2026-10-31 — A scan does not move a peer off a link that is coming up:

  * src/transport/routing.rs and src/transport/registry.rs
      - The registry publishes the devices it is connecting to, handshaking with or
        connected to (`Routing::set_live_devices`), and `note_scan_hint` no longer
        replaces a prefix's device while that device is one of them. An Android
        peripheral re-advertises under a new address as soon as a central connects, and
        following it forgot the link just made. A handshake-verified device still
        overrides (`note_verified_scan_hint`).

The upstream `examples/` directory was intentionally not vendored; it
demonstrates endpoint wiring that this checkpoint does not yet use.

No other upstream source files were modified (src/transport/mod.rs gained the `sim` and
`priority` modules).

2026-11-02 — Rebased onto upstream v0.5.1-beta.7 (and iroh 1.4):

  Upstream ported to iroh 1.0 itself, gave every peer connection a lifecycle
  identity (#20), closes the iroh connections riding a pipe it takes away
  (#18, `transport::conns`), and rebuilds a peer whose pipe we closed ourselves
  instead of tombstoning it (`resume_after_drain`). Those replace the 2026-07
  iroh 1.0 port and parts of the 2026-10 changes above. What remains of ours:

  * Cargo.toml — `blew` is the vendored path crate; `noq-proto` dropped (the
    hook takes `VarInt` from `iroh::endpoint`); `rand` follows upstream.
  * src/transport/conns.rs — the connection registry also remembers which
    watch left a pipe idle (`take_still_idle`), for the linger below.
  * src/transport/hook.rs — `PIPE_IDLE_LINGER`: a pipe is released only after
    its last connection has closed and it then stayed unused for 30 s. The
    link-priority hold (`before_connect` refusal, `after_handshake` refusal,
    tracking for the sweep) is re-applied on upstream's hook.
  * src/transport/priority.rs, src/transport/sim.rs, tests/sim_pairing.rs —
    unchanged in purpose; `sim.rs` follows the new `Advertised` shape and the
    `restart_scan` interface method.
  * src/transport/registry.rs — a send that asks for a new pipe revives a
    `Drained` or `Forgotten` peer (upstream revives only on advertisement);
    the role fix on a central connect, the probe-disconnect guard for a
    `Discovered` peer, the verified-live suppression message, the new-pipe
    send log and the live-devices set routing reads are kept. Our drain-end
    redial of held sends is dropped: upstream holds sends only through a drain
    that will resume, and that resume dials.
  * src/transport/driver.rs — the `ble.connect` span wraps upstream's
    lifecycle-dispatched connect; `CloseChannel` still skips the central
    disconnect on a pipe the peer dialled (`role`).
  * src/transport/transport.rs — construction keeps upstream's rollback and
    bounded steps, and is split again into the radio part and `assemble`
    (shared with the simulated radio); the identity characteristic, probe
    disconnects, link reservation and linger are re-applied.
  * src/transport/pipe.rs, src/transport/l2cap.rs — per-path traffic tallies
    for the `ble.pipe` span (2026-11-02, before the rebase).
